---
import DocsNav from "../../components/DocsNav.astro";
import SiteLayout from "../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro";
import { docs, repositoryUrl } from "../../data/site";
---

<SiteLayout
  title="Architecture | OrbitKV"
  description="OrbitKV ABI8 identities, token and fixed-state transactions, reclamation, and engine boundaries."
  active="Architecture"
>
  <header class="page-hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">Architecture</p>
    <h1>One authority for snapshots, sharing, and reuse.</h1>
    <p>
      OrbitKV is an attention-lifecycle compiler plus a transactional,
      engine-neutral KV block runtime. ABI8 compiles retention semantics into
      immutable token snapshots and separate generation-checked fixed-state
      transactions. SGLang is the first adapter, not a full replacement; it
      still owns tensor allocation, scheduling, kernels, and model execution.
    </p>
  </header>

  <div class="docs-layout">
    <DocsNav active="Architecture" />
    <article class="prose">
      <h2>Pipeline</h2>
      <pre><code>{`Attention / state retention semantics
    -> Retention IR
    -> Lifetime / head stripes
    -> LayoutProgram
    -> PhysicalPlan
    -> immutable SnapshotLease head
    -> tail / COW copy / write intents
    -> completion-gated publication
    -> detach + final-ref certificate
    -> backend ACK + reuse`}</code></pre>

      <h2>Generated layouts</h2>
      <table class="evidence-table-native">
        <thead><tr><th>Semantics</th><th>Layout</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Full</td><td><code>AppendOnly</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Sliding</td><td><code>Periodic</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Sink + Sliding</td><td><code>Pinned + Periodic</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Same Chunk</td><td><code>ResettableArena</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Per-head windows</td><td>Lifetime-normalized stripes</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2>Safety</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Logical identity is separate from physical slot and generation.</li>
        <li>Address programs are the only logical addressing authority.</li>
        <li>Reuse requires semantic death, reader completion, and backend commit.</li>
        <li>Unsupported semantics and engine contracts fail closed.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>ABI8 manager lifecycle</h2>
      <p>
        A request points at an immutable, generation-checked snapshot head.
        <code>prepare_batch</code> keeps a candidate private and returns tail,
        exact COW copy, and fresh-write intents. <code>submit_batch</code>
        validates exact backend receipts. <code>complete_batch</code> publishes
        every request head atomically only after completion evidence validates.
      </p>
      <p>
        Request and Prefix refs, reader pins, and the writer live on the page.
        Detach returns explicit mirror CLEAR/REPLACE actions; a reclamation
        certificate appears only when the final ref is gone. Ambiguous copy,
        launch, or completion outcomes enter fail-stop quarantine.
      </p>

      <h2>Repeated relocation boundary</h2>
      <p>
        For one request-private Full class under full evacuation, the Rust core
        and ABI8 Python runtime are host-tested through two cycles of append,
        disposition mark, relocation, publication, and exact ACK, including an
        append into the first packed publication. The SGLang periodic trigger is
        also host-tested across two successive reclamation boundaries. This does
        not compose every dense-root feature with a packed root: Prefix
        publication, request fork, and shared partial-tail COW on that packed
        root remain unsupported and fail closed before mutation.
      </p>
      <p>
        An engine-neutral CUDA opaque-byte conformance harness is checked in for
        B1, B4, and B32 with repeated cycles, an independent payload oracle,
        257-byte records, real append/copy/consumer streams, event-ordered copy
        and readback, ACK-gated generation reuse, and final drain. All seven H20
        component cases pass at B1/B4/B32. This remains component conformance,
        not sealed L3/L4, performance, or capacity qualification.
      </p>
      <p>
        Separately, the Qwen2.5-0.5B SGLang relocation diagnostic executed on
        an observed H20 and passed 8/8 Naive/Relocate pairs across four
        order-balanced B1/B4 epochs. Every pair is token-exact, each iteration
        reaches two reclamation rounds, the manager drains completely, and
        failure/quarantine counters remain zero. The paired same-policy oracle
        uses FlashInfer; sparse Naive+FA3 is invalid and now fails closed, while
        a separate relocate-only FA3 smoke passed.
      </p>
      <p>
        ABI8 multi-request relocation is host-tested as one scheduler-batch
        transaction. The runtime issues disposition mark, relocation prepare,
        submit, and complete exactly once each for the batch. The adapter
        flattens all token moves into one copy and one completion event; the
        runtime then performs one aggregate registry commit and one aggregate
        request-head replacement. The adapter validates every mirror plan
        before writes, but those writes are not rollback-atomic; all retirements
        are discharged afterward by one aggregate ACK.
      </p>
      <p>
        This guarantee has a deliberate failure boundary. Once disposition mark
        succeeds, any later uncertain prepare, copy, completion, publication,
        mirror, or ACK outcome fail-stops the runtime; it does not roll the batch
        back. A producer-to-copy event exists, but the current copy event is
        eagerly synchronized on the host, so the path provides no asynchronous
        copy/compute overlap. Formal L3/L4 qualification remains pending. The
        available H20 engine record is diagnostic-only: dirty-source, unsealed,
        not independently hardware-attested, <code>qualified=false</code>, and
        <code>performance_go=false</code>.
      </p>

      <h2>Current integration boundary</h2>
      <p>
        The modular Rust token/fixed-state core and exact 40-symbol C ABI8 wire
        retain host L2 GO. In addition, exact source <code>6f62a23</code> has a
        sealed H20 correctness record for the <code>OrbitKVPrefixCache</code>
        path, the sole current formal L4 result: Qwen Full and GPT-OSS Full+SWA
        at B1/B4, 12 matched pairs over three epochs, using official SGLang
        v0.5.17 with page16 BF16 NHD eager FA3. It verifies exact outputs and
        request traces, seeded Prefix hits, final all-free drain, and advancing
        SWA retirement, reclamation, and wrap counters for every Hybrid pair.
        It does not cover relocation, MLA, fixed-state, overlap, Graph,
        speculation, distributed execution, or performance qualification.
      </p>
      <p>
        The exact request-private GDN+convolution runtime is integrated with
        structural admission. Request allocation, initial clear, forward-event
        propagation, release-time wait, retire, clear, and exact ACK are wired.
        Qwen3.5 has six scoped pairs across three epochs; Qwen3.8 has a separate
        diagnostic. Neither result is L4- or performance-qualified. Replacement
        copy is covered only by coordinator and real-CPU-tensor host tests; its
        production trigger remains pending, as do KDA, ShortConv, and other
        family bindings. Manager and state-pool handles are separate and provide
        no cross-handle atomicity.
      </p>

      <h2>Capability levels</h2>
      <p>
        OrbitKV reports compiler, host/ABI, GPU-primitive, engine-E2E,
        and production qualification separately. The{" "}
        <a href={`${repositoryUrl}/blob/main/docs/capability-matrix.md`}>
          Capability Matrix
        </a>{" "}
        is the normative boundary.
      </p>

      <h2>Records</h2>
      <div class="doc-grid">
        {docs.map((item) => (
          <div class="doc-card">
            <span>{item.key}</span>
            <h3>{item.name}</h3>
            <p>{item.detail}</p>
            <a href={item.href}>Open record →</a>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>

      <div class="doc-note">
        <strong>Boundary.</strong>
        <p>
          The sealed ABI8 Prefix record reports mean manager overheads of +7.37%
          and +11.87% for Full B1/B4, and +3.99% and +4.45% for Hybrid B1/B4.
          These are descriptive measurements: <code>performance_go=false</code>,
          with no speedup, memory-saving, or general replacement claim. The
          frozen 9233c06d ABI5-v5 snapshot remains historical scoped L4 evidence
          only. The separate Qwen3.8 diagnostic ran four epochs each at B1 and
          B4 (eight pairs total): tokens are exact, the final census is clean,
          and explicit Triton FP8 is used. Hot throughput is -6.19% at noisy B1
          and -2.70% at B4. Equal configured arena reservation has an observed
          difference of 0%, not a qualified memory result. Its source is dirty
          and unsealed, no qualification preflight ran, and
          <code>hardware_attested=false</code>, <code>qualified=false</code>, and
          <code>performance_go=false</code>; it is not a performance or L4 claim.
          The relocation component has seven passing H20 conformance cases, and
          the Qwen2.5-0.5B engine diagnostic has eight token-exact, census-clean
          pairs. Hot Relocate throughput is +7.63% at B1 and -1.23% at B4, with
          mean latency -7.09% and +1.25% and p95 -22.84% and +2.88%,
          respectively. B1 has substantial epoch jitter, so no speedup claim is
          made. The record remains dirty, unsealed, independently unattested,
          <code>qualified=false</code>, and <code>performance_go=false</code>.
          The batch path is post-mark fail-stop/non-rollback and host-blocking,
          without asynchronous overlap or a capacity claim. MLA, fixed-state L4,
          overlap/Graph, speculation, multi-GPU, and disaggregation remain
          outside the sealed ABI8 record.
        </p>
        <a href={`${repositoryUrl}/blob/main/docs/capability-matrix.md`}>
          Current capability matrix
        </a>
      </div>
    </article>
  </div>
</SiteLayout>
